Another way is to view the valid part of `nums1` followed by `nums2` as one virtual array. For each position, find the smallest value in the remaining part and swap it into that position.

After sorting this virtual range, the values are copied into the full `nums1` buffer. This uses constant extra space, though it is mainly useful for understanding an in-place method because the repeated scans make it slow.

Dry run: for `[3,5]` and `[1,4]`, the first scan finds `1`, the next finds `3`, then `4`, and finally `5`.

```cpp
class Solution {
private:
    int read(const vector<int>& nums1, int m, const vector<int>& nums2, int index) {
        return index < m ? nums1[index] : nums2[index - m];
    }

    void write(vector<int>& nums1, int m, vector<int>& nums2, int index, int value) {
        if (index < m) {
            nums1[index] = value;
        } else {
            nums2[index - m] = value;
        }
    }

public:
    void merge(vector<int>& nums1, int m, vector<int>& nums2, int n) {
        int total = m + n;

        for (int start = 0; start < total; ++start) {
            int smallest = start;

            for (int current = start + 1; current < total; ++current) {
                if (read(nums1, m, nums2, current) < read(nums1, m, nums2, smallest)) {
                    smallest = current;
                }
            }

            if (smallest != start) {
                int firstValue = read(nums1, m, nums2, start);
                int secondValue = read(nums1, m, nums2, smallest);
                write(nums1, m, nums2, start, secondValue);
                write(nums1, m, nums2, smallest, firstValue);
            }
        }

        for (int index = 0; index < total; ++index) {
            nums1[index] = read(nums1, m, nums2, index);
        }
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O((m + n)^2)`.
Space complexity: `O(1)`, not counting the input arrays.

The gap method treats both arrays as one long sorted range. Elements that are `gap` positions apart are compared and swapped. The gap is reduced until it becomes zero, similar to the idea behind Shell sort.

For example, with values `1, 4, 7` and `2, 3, 8`, the first large gap fixes far apart values, and smaller gaps finish the ordering. The code works in place, but it is less direct than the usual backward merge.

```java
class Solution {
    public void merge(int[] nums1, int m, int[] nums2, int n) {
        int total = m + n;
        int gap = (total + 1) / 2;

        while (gap > 0) {
            int left = 0;
            int right = left + gap;

            while (right < total) {
                if (get(nums1, m, nums2, left) > get(nums1, m, nums2, right)) {
                    int temp = get(nums1, m, nums2, left);
                    set(nums1, m, nums2, left, get(nums1, m, nums2, right));
                    set(nums1, m, nums2, right, temp);
                }
                ++left;
                ++right;
            }

            if (gap == 1) {
                break;
            }
            gap = (gap + 1) / 2;
        }

        for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
            nums1[m + i] = nums2[i];
        }

        for (int i = 0; i < total; ++i) {
            if (i >= m) {
                nums1[i] = nums2[i - m];
            }
        }
    }

    private int get(int[] nums1, int m, int[] nums2, int index) {
        return index < m ? nums1[index] : nums2[index - m];
    }

    private void set(int[] nums1, int m, int[] nums2, int index, int value) {
        if (index < m) {
            nums1[index] = value;
        } else {
            nums2[index - m] = value;
        }
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O((m + n) log(m + n))`.
Space complexity: `O(1)`. The original `nums2` values are copied into the unused part of `nums1` after the virtual comparisons.

The free positions in `nums1` are at the end, so filling from the back avoids moving values that are already in place. Compare the largest remaining values from both arrays and write the larger one at the last open position.

This changes `nums1` directly and uses no result array. For a quick dry run, `nums1 = [1,3,5,0,0,0]` and `nums2 = [2,4,6]` place `6`, then `5`, then `4`, and so on from right to left.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    void merge(vector<int>& nums1, int m, vector<int>& nums2, int n) {
        int first = m - 1;
        int second = n - 1;
        int write = m + n - 1;

        while (second >= 0) {
            if (first >= 0 && nums1[first] > nums2[second]) {
                nums1[write] = nums1[first];
                --first;
            } else {
                nums1[write] = nums2[second];
                --second;
            }
            --write;
        }
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(m + n)`.
Space complexity: `O(1)`.

## Intuition
The problem requires merging two sorted arrays into one sorted array in-place, where the first array has enough space to accommodate both. A naive approach would copy elements and sort, but that is inefficient. Instead, we can leverage the fact that both arrays are sorted and the extra space in the first array is at the end. By comparing elements from the end of both arrays and placing the larger element at the end of the first array, we avoid overwriting unprocessed elements.

## Approach
We use three pointers:

* ptr1 starting at the last valid element of nums1

* ptr2 starting at the last element of nums2

* ptr3 starting at the last index of the merged array

While ptr2 is valid, compare the current elements of nums1 and nums2:

* If the current element in nums1 is larger, place it at ptr3 and move ptr1 left.

* Otherwise, place the current element from nums2 at ptr3 and move ptr2 left.

* Move ptr3 left after each placement.

Continue until all elements from nums2 are merged.
## Solution
```java[]
class Solution {
  public void merge(int[] nums1, int m, int[] nums2, int n) {
    int ptr1 = m - 1;
    int ptr2 = n - 1;
    int ptr3 = m + n - 1;

    while (ptr2 >= 0) {
      if (ptr1 >= 0 && nums1[ptr1] > nums2[ptr2]) {
        nums1[ptr3--] = nums1[ptr1--];
      } else {
        nums1[ptr3--] = nums2[ptr2--];
      }
    }
  }
}

```

## Complexity
**Time Complexity: O(m + n)** 
We iterate through both arrays exactly once.

**Space Complexity: O(1)**
We only use a constant amount of extra space for the pointers.

